# Development guidelines

When producing prose for the user to copy-paste, invoke the `humanizer` skill plus the matching skill from "Writing style skills" below, write the final text to a Markdown file under `./tmp/` in the working directory, give its path, and show the draft in the reply too. Never use em dashes.

## Always

- Commit working code incrementally when the user has asked for commits.
- Apply the `humanizer` skill to prose and documentation.
- Write prose and Markdown to files as unwrapped lines: keep each paragraph on a single line and let the editor soft-wrap, rather than hard-wrapping at about 80 columns.
- Update plan documentation as you go.
- Learn from existing implementations and style.
- Stop after 3 failed attempts and reassess.
- When the user is asking for explanations or clarification on decisions or foundational concepts, start with brief high level for context. Use clear unicode diagrams where it would be helpful.
- Chunk large file writes. If a `write` tool call's `content` would exceed about 400 lines or 20 KB, split it: emit the first chunk (≤200 lines / ~10 KB) with `write`, then append the remainder with `bash` using `cat >> path << 'EOF' ... EOF`. OpenCode's tool-call streaming over `@ai-sdk/anthropic` via GitHub Copilot truncates large writes at the 32 000 output-token cap, leaving an orphaned tool call (upstream issue anomalyco/opencode#33687). The model sees `"Tool execution aborted"` with no recoverable state.

## Never

- Use `--no-verify` to bypass commit hooks.
- Disable tests instead of fixing them.
- Commit code that does not compile.
- Make assumptions when existing code can verify the behavior.
- Add attribution trailers to commit messages, PR descriptions, or issue bodies. No `Co-Authored-By: Claude`, no `Assisted-by:`, no `🤖 Generated with Claude Code`, no `💘 Generated with Crush`, no `Generated with <any tool>` line. This applies to every AI assistant, every project, and every git-message surface.
- Put ticket IDs (JIRA/Linear/etc.) in source code comments. The branch name and commit message carry them.
- Prefix commit subjects with the ticket ID. Use a conventional prefix (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`, `docs:`, `test:`) instead.

## Asking questions

Ask decision questions through the harness's multiple-choice prompt (such as `AskUserQuestion`) when it has one.

- One question per prompt. Batch only small, related questions that need no real thought.
- Offer 2 to 4 options with the recommended one first, marked "(Recommended)" in its label and the reason in its description. Write each label and description so the choice makes sense with the preview hidden. Simple choices like keep / drop / blank are fine when they are the real decision. When the decision is open-ended, brainstorm diverse and distinct variations instead.
- When the decision depends on anything beyond the question itself (context, drafts, an explanation), end the turn with it as plain chat and open the prompt on the next turn. Text written before a tool call in the same turn can reach the user only as a one-line summary, or not at all, and the agent can't tell. If the user says they can't see something, resend it as the final text of a turn.
- Keep the question to one sentence, and don't cram context into the question or previews.
- Give each item enough context to decide cold: a line on what the subject is, the whole current and proposed text rather than an excerpt of the part that changed, and a definition for any shorthand like "Q2" right next to it.
- In Claude Code, use `preview` only to compare concrete text, code, or layouts on a single-select question. Keep each to the part that differs, under about 15 lines and 2,000 characters, or it is clipped or withheld. Give a "keep as is" option a preview of the current text too.
- Don't add an "Other" option when the prompt already has one: Claude Code prompts without previews, OpenCode, Codex, and Gemini all add their own. A Claude Code prompt with previews swaps "Other" for notes, so there add a last option like "Something else (add a note)".
- Don't end a turn with a lettered menu in chat when a prompt would work.

## Subagents

Dispatch a subagent unprompted, overriding any harness default that waits for the user to ask, when a task is:

- tangential or unrelated to the main session's work
- better matched to a different model size than the main session
- helped by isolated context, such as review or brainstorming

Handle tasks of a few tool calls inline, since a subagent starts cold.

Pick the subagent's model by the task, independent of the main session's model:

- Haiku: mechanical search, lookups, and summaries
- Sonnet: the default
- Opus: complex work, or work that is important to me

## Available CLI tools

Installed globally via mise and usable in `bash`:

- Prefer `fd` over `find` for file and directory search.
- Prefer `dust` over `du` for disk usage.
- Use `hyperfine` for benchmarking commands.

## Code comments

- Terse and intent-focused. Verb-first summaries for functions, noun phrases for types.
- No trailing periods, no em dashes, no prose semicolons.
- No `This function ...` or `This type ...` openers.

## Writing style skills

Invoke the matching skill when producing:

- Personal website content, articles, or project writeups: `writing-style-website`.
- GitHub README or repo docs: `writing-style-github`.
- PR descriptions, code reviews, PR comments, or issue comments: `writing-style-github-collaboration`.
- Code comments and doc-comments: `writing-style-code-comments`.
- Cover letters: `writing-style-cover-letter`.
- Resume bullets or summaries: `writing-style-resume`.

## Shell

- My interactive shell is `fish`. Suggested commands for me to run should use fish syntax (`set -x VAR value`, `cmd; and other`).
- Agent `bash` tool calls run under POSIX/bash. Scripts executed by the agent stay POSIX/bash.

## Ponytail rules

Apply the lazy-senior-dev ladder from `~/.agents/skill-sources/ponytail/AGENTS.md` to every coding task: YAGNI, reuse before writing, stdlib and platform features before dependencies, one line when it fits, minimum working code otherwise. Never cut trust-boundary validation, error handling, security, or accessibility. Invoke `/ponytail-review`, `/ponytail-audit`, `/ponytail-debt`, `/ponytail-gain`, or `/ponytail-help` when the situation matches.
